---
/**
 * The support page.
 *
 * It exists because the app links to it. `SupportCommands` from
 * `swift-support-kit` renders three Help items — the tracker, the feedback form
 * and `siteURL + supportPath` — and the third needs somewhere to land.
 *
 * It routes rather than explains. Three channels, and the only thing a reader
 * has to get right is which one their problem belongs in: the public tracker for
 * anything about the software, the form for anything that quotes their own
 * servers or profiles, mail for a licence. Everything past that is a link.
 *
 * Structure and wording follow Cupertino's `support.astro`, because the two apps
 * sell the same way and a buyer of both should not have to learn two routes.
 */
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import {
  APP_NAME,
  DOCS,
  FEEDBACK_URL,
  ISSUES_URL,
  PRICE,
  SITE_URL,
  SUPPORT_EMAIL,
  SUPPORT_EMAIL_HREF,
} from "../config";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "ContactPage",
  name: "Support",
  url: `${SITE_URL}/support/`,
  isPartOf: { "@id": `${SITE_URL}/#website` },
  publisher: { "@id": `${SITE_URL}/#organization` },
};

const h2 = "m-0 font-display text-[22px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em]";
const lead = "mt-5 mb-0 max-w-[62ch] text-[15px] leading-[1.65] text-fg-dim text-pretty";
const prose = "mt-4 mb-0 max-w-[62ch] text-[15px] leading-[1.65] text-fg-dim text-pretty";
const link = "underline underline-offset-2";
const strong = "font-medium text-fg";
---

<Layout
  title={`Support — ${APP_NAME}`}
  description={`Get help with ${APP_NAME}: the public issue tracker, a private feedback form for reports that quote your own setup, or email for licences.`}
  schema={schema}
>
  <Nav />
  <main id="main" tabindex="-1" class="wrap py-20">
    <h1 class="m-0 font-display text-[clamp(28px,4.4vw,40px)] font-semibold tracking-[-0.04em]">
      Support
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 mb-0 max-w-[62ch] text-[16px] leading-[1.6] text-fg-dim text-pretty">
      Three ways in, and which one to use comes down to a single question:
      <strong class={strong}>does telling us what went wrong mean quoting your own setup?</strong>
    </p>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class={h2}>A bug, a question, an idea</h2>
      <p class={lead}>
        The <a href={ISSUES_URL} rel="noopener" class={link}>issue tracker</a> is the primary channel
        and the best place for anything that is not about your own servers — a catalog entry that will
        not install, a client that will not wire up, a gateway that will not start, a protocol
        question, a feature request. It is public, so the answer is there for whoever asks next.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class={h2}>Anything that quotes your own setup</h2>
      <p class={lead}>
        A real {APP_NAME} bug report often has to include a server's error output, a profile or host
        name, or a row from the activity log. The tracker is public and permanent; the
        <a href={FEEDBACK_URL} class={link}>feedback form</a> is neither. No account is needed and the
        email address is optional — leave it blank and the report is anonymous.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        <strong class={strong}>Choosing Send Feedback in the app does not send anything.</strong> It
        hands your browser a URL and stops. Everything that URL carries — the app version, the macOS
        version, the Mac model and the language — is visible in the address bar, and on the page as
        four fields you can edit or clear before pressing send.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        Leave credentials out of every channel. Nobody here needs one to answer you, and {APP_NAME}
        cannot show you a secret once it is set, so there is nothing to copy by accident.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class={h2}>Licences and payment</h2>
      <p class={lead}>
        Email <a href={SUPPORT_EMAIL_HREF} class={link}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a> for a key that will not
        activate, a key you have lost, or a refund. {PRICE.refund}. A key is checked on your Mac and
        never sent anywhere, so a reply is the only way we can look at one — quote the address you
        bought with rather than the key itself.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        What a key covers, and what the trial does, is set out in the
        <a href="/terms" class={link}>licence terms</a> and in
        <a href={DOCS.licensing} rel="noopener" class={link}>docs/licensing.md</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class={h2}>Finding the Help menu</h2>
      <p class={lead}>
        {APP_NAME} lives in the menu bar, so it only has an app menu while one of its windows is open.
        Choose <strong class={strong}>Open {APP_NAME}</strong> from the menu bar panel and Help ▸ Send
        Feedback is there. Opening this page or the form directly in a browser works exactly as well
        — the app only ever hands them over.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class={h2}>What to expect</h2>
      <p class={lead}>
        Everything gets read, and most things get an answer within a few working days. A report that
        says which version, which macOS, which client and which server was involved is one that can
        usually be answered in the first reply rather than the third.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
